bf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal.\n\nNow, substituting the change of variable\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\ninto our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{T}Ax &= (Py)^{T}A(Py) \\\\\n&= y^{T}(P^{T}AP)y \\\\\n&= y^{T}Dy\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form.\n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A$, we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}$.\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}1 - λ & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\n(1 - λ)(1 - λ) - 4 &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 2λ - 3 &= 0 \\\\\n(λ + 1)(λ - 3) &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 3,-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163235 1064 "Consider a quadratic form \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is a symmetric matrix. As we know, any symmetric matrix can be orthogonally diagonalized as follows:\n\n$$\nD = P^T \\! AP,\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is an orthogonal matrix and $D$ is a diagonal matrix that contains eigenvalues of $A$ on the main diagonal. \n\nNow, substituting the change of variable $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}$ into our quadratic form, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{T}\\,Ax & =(Py)^{T}\\,A(Py) \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}\\,AP)y \\\\  & ={y}^{T}\\,Dy,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives us a canonical form. \n\nAs a result, using the orthogonal diagonalization of $A,$ we can find the canonical form of $\\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x}.$\n\nFor example, let's find the canonical form of \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2+4x_1x_2+x_2^2.\n$$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTo find the coefficients of the canonical form, we need to calculate the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}1-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 1-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (1-\\lambda )(1-\\lambda )-4 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-2\\lambda -3 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda +1)(\\lambda -3) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =3,\\,-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, writing the eigenvalues in descending order, we obtain the diagonal matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe corresponding quadratic form after a change of variables is\n\n$$\n3y_1^2 - y_2^2.\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163237 1064 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y}$, where $D$ is a diagonal matrix.\n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A$, which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation\n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form.\n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mid A - λI \\mid &= 0 \\\\\n\\begin{vmatrix}6 - λ & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3 - λ\\end{vmatrix} &= 0 \\\\\nλ^{2} - 9λ + 14 &= 0 \\\\\nλ &= 7, 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1 \\| = \\sqrt{5}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2 \\| = \\sqrt{5}$, we get\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$ and $\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}$\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2$.\n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to\n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2\n$$\n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n7y_{1}^{2} + 2y_{2}^{2} &= 14 \\\\\n(y_{1}^{2})/((\\sqrt{2})^{2}) + (y_{2}^{2})/((\\sqrt{7})^{2}) &= 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}$." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163237 1064 "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ symmetric matrix with real entries. As we know, there exists an orthonormal change of variables \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{x}=P\\mathbf{y}\n$$\n\nthat transforms the quadratic form $\\mathbf{x}^TA\\mathbf{x}$ into the quadratic form $\\mathbf{y}^TD\\mathbf{y},$ where $D$ is a diagonal matrix. \n\nThe columns of the matrix $P$ are the (normalized) unit eigenvectors of $A,$ which are mutually orthogonal. These are called the **principal axes** of the quadratic form.\n\nLet's use this knowledge to find out which curve is defined by the equation \n\n$$\n6x_1^2+4x_1x_2+3x_2^2= 14,\n$$\n\nand see the geometric interpretation of the principal axes.\n\nIf we plot our curve using a graphical calculator, we obtain the following:\n\n![](images/d8/d8a6b6dff52e0c4004432f9447b5fd484612eaacf07588c3469af143b0a9e880.png)\n\nIt looks like an ellipse. Let's now find our coordinate system, in which the equation of the curve will be the simplest.\n\nFirst, we consider the corresponding quadratic form \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})={\\color{blue}6}x_1^2+{\\color{red}4}x_1x_2+{\\color{blue}3}x_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich we reduce to its canonical (diagonal) form. \n\nSo, we write down the matrix of $Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}6 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNext, we find the eigenvalues of $A\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}6-\\lambda  & 2 \\\\ 2 & 3-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-9\\lambda +14 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =7,\\,2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's find the principal axes of the curve.\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-7I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_1$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_1  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- Seeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ Dividing $\\mathbf{v}_2$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v}_2  \\| = \\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nTherefore, the principal axes of our quadratic form (and the corresponding curve) are\n\n$\\qquad$ $[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$\n\nNotice that in the diagram below, the principal axes correspond to the axes $y_1$ and $y_2.$ \n\n![](images/10/10a2f0ccd179eba7f65410f932548d954a459e1bc8dcb0803a5b2d9fe7896432.png)\n\nIn the new coordinate system, our quadratic form can be reduced to \n\n$$\n7y_1^2 + 2y_2^2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the curve has the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{y}_{1}^{2}+2{y}_{2}^{2} & =14 \\\\ \\frac{{y}_{1}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{2})^{2}}+\\frac{{y}_{2}^{2}}{(\\sqrt{7})^{2}} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $\\sqrt2$ and $\\sqrt{7}.$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163244 1064 "**Lagrange's method** can be used to reduce any quadratic form to a canonical form by completing the squares and applying changes of variables.\n\nTo see how it works, let's reduce\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 +5x_2^2 + 4x_1x_2-2x_1x_3-4x_2x_3\n$$\n\nto its canonical form.\n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nQ(x) &= x_{1}^{2} + 5x_{2}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} - 2x_{1}x_{3} - 4x_{2}x_{3} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 4x_{1}x_{2} - 2x_{1}x_{3}) - 4x_{2}x_{3} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(2x_{2} - x_{3})) - 4x_{2}x_{3} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1}^{2} + 2x_{1}(2x_{2} - x_{3}) + (2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2} - (2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2}) - 4x_{2}x_{3} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2} - (2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2} - 4x_{2}x_{3} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2} - 4x_{2}^{2} + 4x_{2}x_{3} - x_{3}^{2} - 4x_{2}x_{3} + 5x_{2}^{2} \\\\\n&= (x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3})^{2} + x_{2}^{2} - x_{3}^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\ny_1^2 + y_2^2 - y_3^2\n$$\n\nwhere we performed the following substitution:\n\n$$\n{y_{1} = x_{1} + 2x_{2} - x_{3}; y_{2} = x_{2}; y_{3} = x_{3}\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163244 1064 "**Lagrange's method** can be used to reduce any quadratic form to a canonical form by completing the squares and applying changes of variables. \n\nTo see how it works, let's reduce \n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2 +5x_2^2 + 4x_1x_2-2x_1x_3-4x_2x_3\n$$\n\n to its canonical form. \n\nFirst, we group $x_1^2$-term with all cross-product terms that contain $x_1$ and complete the square inside the parentheses:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q(x) & ={x}_{1}^{2}+5{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3})-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3}))-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}^{2}+2{x}_{1}(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})+(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2})-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-(2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}+4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}-{x}_{3}^{2}-4{x}_{2}{x}_{3}+5{x}_{2}^{2} \\\\  & =({x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3})^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}-{x}_{3}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, the canonical form is\n\n$$\ny_1^2 + y_2^2 - y_3^2,\n$$\n\nwhere we performed the following substitution:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{y}_{1}={x}_{1}+2{x}_{2}-{x}_{3} \\\\ {y}_{2}={x}_{2} \\\\ {y}_{3}={x}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163255 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A$.\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A$.\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P$.\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.** We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0$:\n$\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2$\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3$. Seeking a nonzero solution of $\\mid A-\\lambda_i I \\mid = 0$ for $i=1,2,3$, we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n$e_{2}|= v_{2} - \\frac{v_{2} \\cdot e_{1}}{e_{1} \\cdot e_{1}}e_{1}; = v_{2} - \\frac{1}{2}e_{1}; = \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} - (-\\frac{1}{2})\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}; = \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}$\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3$. Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n$\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163255 1064 "Let's recall the algorithm for diagonalizing a $3\\times 3$ symmetric matrix $A.$\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^3$ consisting of unit orthogonal eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nNote the following important points regarding step 2 of this process:\n\n- If $A$ has mutually distinct eigenvalues, the eigenvectors will automatically be orthogonal, so we only need to normalize them.\n\n- If $A$ has *repeated* eigenvalues, then the eigenvectors corresponding to these repeated eigenvalues *may not* be orthogonal. Therefore, we have to apply the Gram-Schmidt process to obtain orthogonal vectors.\n\nFor example, consider the following matrix:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 2 \\\\ -2 & 2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix is symmetric. Let's apply our diagonalization algorithm.\n\n- **Step 1.**  We find the eigenvalues of $A$ by solving $\\det(A-\\lambda I)=0\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=4, \\qquad \\lambda_2=4, \\qquad \\lambda_3=-2\n$$\n\n- **Step 2.** Now, we need to find an eigenvector basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Seeking a nonzero solution of $|A-\\lambda_i I| = 0$ for $i=1,2,3,$ we get the corresponding eigenvectors\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that $\\mathbf{v}_1$ and $\\mathbf{v}_2$ are not orthogonal. So, we proceed using the Gram-Schmidt process. We set \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nand we find $\\mathbf{e}_2$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}_{2} & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{{v}_{2}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{{e}_{1}\\cdot {e}_{1}}{e}_{1} \\\\  & ={v}_{2}-\\frac{1}{2}{e}_{1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]-(-\\frac{1}{2})[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the vectors $\\mathbf e_1, \\mathbf e_2$ and $\\mathbf v_3$ form an *orthogonal* basis of $\\mathbb R^3.$ Dividing each eigenvector by its norm, we obtain the unit orthogonal eigenvectors:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\n- **Step 3.** We construct the diagonal matrix $D$ and the invertible matrix $P$ as follows:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{aligned}]\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163262 1064 "In some special cases, a quadratic equation might generate a degenerate (real) curve or even an empty set.\n\n- For example, consider the quadratic equation $-3x_1^2+8x_1x_2+3x_2^2 = 0$.\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n$5y_1^2 - 5y_2^2 = 0$\nwhich represents two intersecting lines $y_1 = y_2$ and $y_1 = -y_2$.\n\n- Now consider the quadratic equation $-2x_1^2+8x_1x_2-8x_2^2 = 10$.\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n$-10y_2^2 =10$\nwhich represents an empty set as the equation $y_2^2 = -1$ does not have any real solution.\n- Finally, reducing the left-hand side of $x_1^2-4x_1x_2+4x_2^2 = 5$\nto the canonical form, we obtain\n$5y_2^2 =5$\nwhich represents a pair of parallel lines $y_2 = 1$ and $y_2=-1$." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163262 1064 "In some special cases, a quadratic equation might generate a degenerate curve or even an empty set.\n\n- For example, consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-3x_1^2+8x_1x_2+3x_2^2 = 0.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_1^2 - 5y_2^2 = 0\n$$\n\nwhich represents two intersecting lines $y_1 = y_2$ and $y_1 = -y_2.$\n\n- Now consider the quadratic equation \n\n$$\n-2x_1^2+8x_1x_2-8x_2^2 = 10.\n$$\n\nReducing the left-hand side to the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n- 10y_2^2 =10\n$$\n\nwhich represents an empty set as the equation $y_2^2 = -1$ does not have any real solution.\n\n- Finally, reducing the left-hand side of \n\n$$\nx_1^2-4x_1x_2+4x_2^2 = 5\n$$\n\nto the canonical form, we obtain\n\n$$\n5y_2^2 =5\n$$\n\nwhich represents a pair of parallel lines $y_2 = 1$ and $y_2=-1.$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163263 1064 "Consider the quadratic function\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2+2x_1x_2\n$$\n\nUsing Lagrange's method, we can find its canonical form\n\n$$\ny_1^2 - 3y_2^2\n$$\n\nwhere\n\n$$\ny_1 = x_1+x_2\n$$\n\nand $y_2 = x_2$.\n\n**Watch out!** This is not the only possible canonical form! For example, substituting\n\n$$\n{z_{1} = y_{1}; z_{2} = \\sqrt{3}y_{2}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n$z_1^2-z_2^2$,\nwhich is another canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x})$. In conclusion, the canonical (diagonal) form is *not* unique.\n\nOn the other hand, among all the possibilities, there is one special canonical form.\n\nThe **normal form** of a quadratic function is a canonical form where all coefficients are either $1$ or $-1$.\n\nThe normal form is unique (up to the order of the terms) for each quadratic function." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163263 1064 "Consider the quadratic function\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x})=x_1^2-2x_2^2+2x_1x_2.\n$$\n\nUsing Lagrange's method, we can find its canonical form\n\n$$\ny_1^2 - 3y_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhere $y_1 = x_1+x_2$ and $y_2 = x_2.$\n\n**Watch out!** This is not the only possible canonical form! For example, substituting \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{z}_{1}={y}_{1} \\\\ {z}_{2}=\\sqrt{3}{y}_{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\nz_1^2-z_2^2,\n$$\n\nwhich is another canonical form of $Q(\\mathbf{x}).$ In conclusion, the canonical (diagonal) form is *not* unique. \n\nOn the other hand, among all the possibilities, there is one special canonical form. \n\nThe **normal form** of a quadratic function is a canonical form where all coefficients are either $1$ or $-1.$\n\nThe normal form is unique (up to the order of the terms) for each quadratic function." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163283 1064 "Let's now consider Jordan canonical forms of $3 \\times 3$ matrices.\n\nLet $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The simplest case is when $A$ has three distinct eigenvalues, say $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2$, and $\\lambda_3$. In this case, $A$ must be diagonalizable. Therefore, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}λ_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & λ_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & λ_{3}\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n$\\begin{bmatrix}7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 9\\end{bmatrix}$\nThis matrix has eigenvalues\n\n$$\n\\lambda_1=8\n$$\n\n$$\n\\lambda_2=5\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\lambda_3=3\n$$\n\nTherefore, its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nRemember that a Jordan canonical form of a matrix is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163283 1064 "Let's now consider Jordan canonical forms of $3 \\times 3$ matrices.\n\nLet $A$ be a $3\\times 3$ matrix. The simplest case is when $A$ has three distinct eigenvalues, say $\\lambda_1, \\lambda_2,$ and  $\\lambda_3.$ In this case, $A$ must be diagonalizable. Therefore, the Jordan canonical form of $A$ is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{\\lambda }_{1} & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & {\\lambda }_{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & {\\lambda }_{3}\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the matrix\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}7 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 8 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -4 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis matrix has eigenvalues $\\lambda_1=8,$ $\\lambda_2=5,$ and $\\lambda_3=3.$ Therefore, its Jordan canonical form is\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}8 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nRemember that a Jordan canonical form of a matrix is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186163284 1064 "Let's now consider the matrix $A$, given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-8)^2(\\lambda - 3) = 0\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix has only two distinct eigenvalues, namely,\n\n- $\\lambda=8$ of algebraic multiplicity $2$, and\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1$.\n\nHow can we find its Jordan canonical form?\n\nIn this example, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3$.\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to\n\n$$\n\\lambda=8{:}\n$$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(8)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chain:\n$\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{0}$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(8) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(8)$. This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chains:\n$v_{1}→^(A - 8 I)0; v_{2}→^(A - 8 I)0$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find the number of linearly independent eigenvectors corresponding to $\\lambda=8$.\n\nComputing $A-8I$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 8I &= \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 9\\end{bmatrix} - 8\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} = \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we reduce this matrix to row echelon form using Gaussian elimination, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA - 8I &= \\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{vmatrix}R_{1}↔R_{2} \\\\ ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ -4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{vmatrix}R_{2}:=R_{2} + 4R_{1} \\\\\n&= R_{3}:=R_{3} + (-6)R_{1} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\mid R_{3}:=R_{3} + R_{2} \\\\\n&= ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5 & 5 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\text{nullity}(A-8I)=1\n$$\n\nthere will be only one eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$, say, $\\mathbf{v}_1$. So, the generalized eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have *case I*, and $A$ has the following Jordan canonical form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 8 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nAs always, the Jordan canonical form is unique up to the order of the Jordan blocks." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186163284 1064 "Let's now consider the matrix $A,$ given by\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}4 & 3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 6 & 1 \\\\ 6 & -7 & 9\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThe characteristic equation of $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n(\\lambda-8)^2(\\lambda - 3) = 0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, this matrix has only two distinct eigenvalues, namely,\n\n- $\\lambda=8$ of algebraic multiplicity $2,$ and\n\n- $\\lambda=3$ of algebraic multiplicity $1.$\n\nHow can we find its Jordan canonical form?\n\nIn this example, we have one $1 \\times 1$ Jordan block $J_1(3)$ corresponding to $\\lambda=3.$\nHowever, there are two possibilities for the Jordan blocks corresponding to $\\lambda=8{:}$\n\n- *Case I.* We may get the single $2 \\times 2$ Jordan block $J_2(8).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chain:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{v}_2 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{v}_1 \\xrightarrow{A-8I} \\mathbf{0}\n$$\n\n- *Case II.* We may get a composition of two $1 \\times 1$ Jordan blocks, namely $J_1(8) {\\:\\oplus\\:} J_1(8).$ This happens when the generalized eigenvectors $\\mathbf v_1$ and $\\mathbf v_2$ corresponding to the eigenvalue $\\lambda=8$ form the following chains:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{v}_{1}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }0 &  \\\\ {v}_{2}\\overset{A-8I\\,}{\\to }0 & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo figure out which of the cases we obtain in this situation, let's find th