r $\\lambda \\neq 0,$ we set $y = e^{m x}.$ The auxiliary equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-18\\lambda m+81{\\lambda }^{2} & =0 \\\\ (m-9\\lambda )(m-9\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives a repeated root $m = 9\\lambda.$ The general solution is\n\n$$\ny = Ae^{9\\lambda x} + Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- Applying the first boundary condition, $y(0) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\ny(0) = Ae^{0} + B(0)e^{0} = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad A = 0.\n$$\n\nSo, the solution simplifies to $y = Bxe^{9\\lambda x}.$ Its derivative is\n\n$$\ny' = Be^{9\\lambda x} + 9B\\lambda xe^{9\\lambda x}.\n$$\n\n- Applying the second boundary condition, $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0,$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(3B{e}^{27\\lambda })+5(B{e}^{27\\lambda }+27B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda }) & =0 \\\\ 12B{e}^{27\\lambda }+5B{e}^{27\\lambda }+135B\\lambda {e}^{27\\lambda } & =0 \\\\ B{e}^{27\\lambda }(17+135\\lambda ) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor a nontrivial solution (an eigenfunction), we require $B\\neq 0.$ Since $e^{27\\lambda}$ is never zero, we must have\n\n$$\n17+135\\lambda = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad \\lambda = -\\dfrac{17}{135}.\n$$\n\nThis is our **eigenvalue**. The corresponding **eigenfunction** is\n\n$$\ny(x) = Bxe^{9(-17/135) x} = Bxe^{-17x/15}.\n$$\n\nNote that any nonzero constant multiple of this function is also an eigenfunction." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186168062 1064 "We begin by considering  the case $\\lambda = 0.$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation, $y'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y  = 0,$ gives a much simpler equation:\n\n$$\ny'' = 0.\n$$\n\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B.\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions. \n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0,$ implies:\n\n$$\ny(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0.\n$$\n\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax.$\n\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A.$ Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(A\\cdot 3)+5(A) & =0 \\\\ 12A+5A & =0 \\\\ 17A & =0\\,\\,⟹\\,\\,A=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0,$ we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0.$ By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue. \n\nNext, we will check the case where $\\lambda \\neq 0.$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186168062 1064 "We begin by considering the case\n\n$$\n\\lambda = 0\n$$\n\nSetting $\\lambda = 0$ in the original equation,\n\n$$\ny'' - 18\\lambda y' + 81\\lambda^2y = 0\n$$\n\ngives a much simpler equation:\n$y'' = 0$.\nIntegrating this equation twice with respect to $x$ gives the general solution\n\n$$\ny = Ax + B\n$$\n\nNow we apply the boundary conditions.\n\n- The first condition, $y(0) = 0$, implies:\n$y(0) = A(0) + B = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad B=0$.\nSo the solution must be of the form $y = Ax$.\n- For the second condition, we first find the derivative, $y' = A$. Applying the condition $4y(3) + 5y'(3) = 0$ yields\n$4(A \\cdot 3) + 5(A)|= 0; 12A + 5A|= 0; 17A|= 0⟹A = 0$.\n\nSince both $A=0$ and $B=0$, we are left with the trivial solution $y \\equiv 0$. By definition, an eigenfunction must be a *nontrivial* solution.\n\nTherefore, $\\lambda = 0$ is *not* an eigenvalue.\n\nNext, we will check the case where\n\n$$\n\\lambda \\neq 0\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186168121 1064 "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve.\n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince\n\n$$\n\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N\n$$\n\nwe can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$, and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186168121 1064 "The **binormal vector** to a curve $C$ defined by the vector-valued function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$ is defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{B}(t) = \\mathbf{T}(t) \\times \\mathbf{N}(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{T}$ and $\\mathbf{N}$ denote the unit tangent and principal normal vectors, respectively.\n\nThe diagram below shows the binormal vector to a curve at some point $P$ on the curve. \n\nTo demonstrate, consider the curve\n\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r}(t) = \\left\\langle 2\\sin t, \\: 2\\cos t, \\: t \\right\\rangle.\n\\]\n\nAt [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}6,[/math] the point on the curve, unit tangent, and principal normal are, respectively, given by\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\left\\langle 1, -\\sqrt3, \\dfrac{5\\pi}{6} \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle, \\\\[5pt]\n\n\\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) &= \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align*}\n\nUsing the formula to calculate the binormal vector, we have\n\n\\[\n\\begin{align}\n\\mathbf{B}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right)\t&= \\mathbf{T}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\times \\mathbf{N}\\left(\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}\\right) \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt5}\\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, 1 \\right\\rangle \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\\left\\langle -1, \\sqrt3, 0 \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\, \\begin{array}{|ccc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\mathbf i & \\mathbf j & \\mathbf k \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5}\\left[ \\ \\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\sqrt3 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf i -\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & 1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & 0\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf j +\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\begin{array}{|cc|}\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-\\sqrt3 & -1 \\\\\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t-1 & \\sqrt3\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\t\\end{array} \\,\\mathbf k \\ \\right] \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle (0-\\sqrt3), -(0-(-1)), (-3-1) \\right\\rangle \\\\[3pt]\n\t\t\t\t\t\t\t&= \\dfrac{1}{2\\sqrt5} \\left\\langle -\\sqrt3, -1, -4 \\right\\rangle.\n\\end{align}\n\\]\n\nBelow is a diagram of the curve [math]\\mathbf{r}(t),[/math] along with its tangent, normal, and binormal vectors at [math]t=\\dfrac{5\\pi}{6}.[/math] Notice that [math]\\mathbf{T}[/math] and [math]\\mathbf{N}[/math] are both orthogonal to [math]\\mathbf{B}.[/math]\n\n![](images/95/95a892eb30b26d1255d29dcaaac552663443c00b2db6fe1e9168a77be061f2c0.png)\n\nSince $\\mathbf B = \\mathbf T\\times \\mathbf N,$ we can conclude that $\\mathbf B$ is perpendicular to both $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N,$ and it's pointing downward.\n\nAlso, since $\\mathbf T$ and $\\mathbf N$ are perpendicular *unit* vectors, it follows that $\\mathbf B$ is also a unit vector." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186168155 1064 "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n> *Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D)$. If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D$, then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n> $f_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A$." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186168155 1064 "Recall that the average value of a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $D = [a,b]$ is given by\n\n$$\nf_\\textrm{ave} = \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Length}(D)}\\int_a^b f(x) \\: \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nThere is an analogous definition for functions of two variables, stated below:\n\n> *Suppose $D$ is a closed and bounded region in $\\mathbb{R}^2$ which has non-zero finite area $\\textrm{Area}(D).$ If $f (x, y)$ is a continuous function on $D,$ then **the average value of $\\boldsymbol f$ on $\\boldsymbol D$** is given by*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> f_{\\textrm{ave}} =\\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A.\n> $$\n\nFor example, let's find the average value of the function \n\n\\[\nf(x,y) =x+3y^2+2\n\\]\n\nover the region \n\n\\[\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n\\] \n\nEarlier, we found that \n\n\\[\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = 28, \\qquad \\textrm{Area}(D) = 4.\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the average value of the function over [math]D[/math] is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nf_{\\textrm{ave}}  \t\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{\\textrm{Area}(D)} \\, \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A \\\\[5pt]\n\n&= \\dfrac{1}{4} \\cdot 28 \\\\[5pt]\n\n&=  7. \n\\end{align*}" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186168156 1064 "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b]$, the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a)\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n> *If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D$, then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n> $\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D)$.\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D$. In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b)$.\n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186168156 1064 "Given a continuous function $f(x)$ on a closed interval $[a, b],$ the mean value theorem for integrals guarantees that there exists a number $c \\in [a,b]$ such that\n\n$$\n\\int_a^b f(x)\\, \\textrm d x = f(c) \\cdot (b-a),\n$$\n\nwhich can be visualized as shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0afb9b15787eb89f73f322290bdbe2288027c54924d274c438773ce151a17291.png)\n\nWe have a similar theorem for functions with two variables. The so-called **mean value theorem for double integrals** states:\n\n> *If the function $f(x,y)$ is continuous on a closed and bounded region $D,$ then there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that*\n> \n> \n> $$\n> \\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n> $$\n\nThis has a nice geometrical interpretation when $f$ is non-negative on $D.$ In this case, the theorem tells us that there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that the volume of the solid enclosed between the surface $z=f(x,y)$ and the $xy$-plane over the region $D$ equals the volume of the right cylinder with base $D$ and height $f(a,b).$ \n\nThe diagram below shows the case when $D$ is rectangular, and the corresponding cylinder is a rectangular solid.\n\n![](images/8e/8ed477207ee61fbdaf963ed8a127e3f1d4cf76707d2eeb4b450fd49f78e15b7e.png)\n\nIn general, the point $(a,b)$ mentioned in the theorem is not unique. Let's see an example." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186168157 1064 "Consider the continuous function\n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{(x,y) \\:: \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D)\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n∬_(D)f(x, y)dA &= ∬_(D)(x + 3y^{2} + 2)dA \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}\\int_{0}^{2}(x + 3y^{2} + 2)dydx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}[xy + y^{3} + 2y]_{y = 0}^{y = 2}dx \\\\\n&= \\int_{0}^{2}(2x + 12)dx \\\\\n&= [x^{2} + 12x]_{x = 0}^{x = 2} \\\\\n&= 28\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n28 &= f(a, b) \\cdot 4 \\\\\n7 &= f(a, b) \\\\\n7 &= a + 3b^{2} + 2 \\\\\na &= 5 - 3b^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example,\n\n$$\nb=1 \\in [0,2]\n$$\n\nand obtain $a=2$. So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186168157 1064 "Consider the continuous function \n\n$$\nf(x,y) = x+3y^2+2\n$$\n\nand the region $D$ given by\n\n$$\nD = \\big\\{ (x,y) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq y \\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the mean value theorem for double integrals, there exists a point $(a,b) \\in D$ such that\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D f(x,y) \\: \\textrm{d}A = f(a,b) \\cdot \\textrm{Area}(D).\n$$\n\nEvaluating the integral on the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{D}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(x+3{y}^{2}+2)\\,\\text{d}y\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{[xy+{y}^{3}+2y]}_{y=0}^{y=2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}(2x+12)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={[{x}^{2}+12x]}_{x=0}^{x=2} \\\\  & =28.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Area}(D) = 2 \\cdot 2 = 4.\n$$\n\nSo, our mean-value equation simplifies to\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}28 & =f(a,b)\\cdot 4 \\\\ 7 & =f(a,b) \\\\ 7 & =a+3{b}^{2}+2 \\\\ a & =5-3{b}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation describes part of a parabola in the $xy$-plane, as shown below.\n\n![](images/46/46873c4ecb290380488e589082fc0759324d3540494f5b83e2d64db5d9cdf62a.png)\n\nTo find a particular point that satisfies the mean value theorem, we can set, for example, $b=1 \\in [0,2]$ and obtain $a=2.$ So, the point $(2,1)$ satisfies the mean value theorem for double integrals. Consequently, the two solids below must have the same volume.\n\n![](images/82/82d3a762ab323339570e6010038e75ce95f3fdbde958a2ffa876972cbf91e8fb.png)" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173160 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$$\nA=PDP^T,\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP.\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A,$ we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n> *A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix \n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & ={{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{P}{{[\\begin{aligned}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{D}{{{[\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{aligned}]}^{T}}_{⏟}}_{{P}^{T}},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A.$ Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A.$\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of  $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A.$\n\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P.$\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173160 1064 "A square matrix $A$ is **orthogonally diagonalizable** if there exists an orthogonal matrix $P$ such that\n\n$A=PDP^T$,\nwhere $D$ is a diagonal matrix. Note that this is equivalent to the equation\n\n$$\nD = P^TAP\n$$\n\nSo, to orthogonally diagonalize a matrix $A$, we need to find a diagonal matrix $D$ and an orthogonal matrix $P$ that satisfies the above equations.\n\nA helpful theorem allows us to determine whether a real matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable. It states the following:\n\n> *A matrix $A$ with real entries is orthogonally diagonalizable if and only if it is symmetric.*\n\nConsequently, *every* symmetric matrix is orthogonally diagonalizable!\n\nFor example, the symmetric matrix\n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nhas the orthogonal diagonalization\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= \\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(P)\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -6\\end{bmatrix}_(⏟)_(D)\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}} & -\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{10}}\\end{bmatrix}^{T}_(⏟)_(P^{T})\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $P$ is orthogonal, and $D$ is diagonal. As usual, the entries on the main diagonal of $D$ are the eigenvalues of $A$. Also, the columns of $P$ are *orthonormal* eigenvectors corresponding to the eigenvalues of $A$.\n\nThe procedure of the orthogonal diagonalization of a $2 \\times 2$ real symmetric matrix $A$ is almost the same as the diagonalization of a matrix:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the eigenvalues of the matrix $A$.\n- **Step 2**: Find a basis of $\\mathbb{R}^2$ consisting of *unit orthogonal* eigenvectors of $A$.\n- **Step 3**: Construct the matrices $D$ and $P$.\n\nIn the case of symmetric matrices, the two eigenvectors we compute in step 2 will always be orthogonal, provided that they correspond to different eigenvalues. However, we must also ensure that we *normalize* those eigenvectors." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173170 1064 "A region $D$ is **connected** if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path that lies entirely in $D$.\n\nThe region below is connected because a smooth path can join any two points.\n\n![](images/90/90fea89e0dabec960edf7eef3552e10cdbccaee80f9ef6ca3d5b2a5b457e6b89.png)\n\nThe region below is not connected. If we take two points $P$ and $Q$, one in each subregion, we cannot find a path joining these points that lies entirely in the region.\n\n![](images/08/08cd555c38106ec22f2ffe4808a6b62e8f72ffda7baf953836beeca5ee6a1faf.png)" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173170 1064 "A region $D$ is **connected** if every pair of points in $D$ can be joined by a smooth curve or a path that lies entirely in $D.$\n\nThe region below is connected because a smooth path can join any two points.\n\n![](images/90/90fea89e0dabec960edf7eef3552e10cdbccaee80f9ef6ca3d5b2a5b457e6b89.png)\n\nThe region below is not connected. If we take two points $P$ and $Q,$ one in each subregion, we cannot find a path joining these points that lies entirely in the region.\n\n![](images/08/08cd555c38106ec22f2ffe4808a6b62e8f72ffda7baf953836beeca5ee6a1faf.png)" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173181 1064 "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2]$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2]$.\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0$, we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}$.![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n$\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle, \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$.\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2]$, these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle, \\quad x \\in [0,2], \\quad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173181 1064 "Consider the planar curve $y=e^{-x}$ defined on the interval $x \\in [0,2],$ as shown below.\n\n![](images/a8/a8ce2449af085548fecc543fbd5ccfec0e8024344547b2a7825f3a6e9ccf63d4.png)\n\nBy rotating this curve $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, we get a **surface of revolution**:\n\n![](images/88/88c9c1ec629ab9967cc011aaadafebea255cd50abee9c3730bed4f53f671c71b.png)\n\nWe can parametrize this surface in the following way:\n\n- We take a point $P$ on the surface with $x$-coordinate equal to $x_0$ for some fixed $x_0 \\in [0,2].$\n\n- Now, by taking the intersection of the surface with the plane $x=x_0,$ we get a circle of radius $e^{-x_0}.$\n\n![](images/f1/f1e2cf1c571a21e06f1b25f99a64b5529fea8c65c897b3f225cfd0d307d725d7.png)\n\n \nThis circle can be parameterized as follows:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(x_0, \\theta) = \\big\\langle {\\color{black}x_0}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x_0} \\, \\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad  \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n- Finally, by allowing $x_0$ to vary over the interval $[0, 2],$ these circles will trace out the surface of revolution. Therefore, our parameterization is\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,\\theta) = \\big\\langle x, \\: e^{-x} \\cos{\\theta}, \\: e^{-x}\\sin{\\theta} \\big\\rangle,\n\\quad \nx \\in [0,2], \n\\quad\n\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nLet's now describe some general formulas for parametrizing surfaces of revolution." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173182 1064 "Recall that if $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the *spherical* polar coordinates of a point $P$ in the three-dimensional space, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\ny = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad\nz = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\n![](images/92/925aae5386657b35b3214db2a5348fc88872faec5ef63bc6237deed6e041f3de.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's find a parametrization of the sphere whose cartesian equation is \n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1.\n$$\n\nThis equation represents a unit sphere (i.e., a sphere of radius $1$) centered at the origin.\n\nGiven a point $P$ on the sphere, the variable $\\rho$ represents the *radial* distance between the origin and $P.$ Since every point on the sphere is a distance of $1$ from $O,$  we must have $\\rho =1$ at every point on the sphere.\n\nTherefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\theta ,ϕ) & =⟨x,y,z⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\rho \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,\\rho \\operatorname{cos}ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,1\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}ϕ⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\operatorname{sin}ϕ,\\,\\operatorname{cos}ϕ⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi].$\nAs a result, the vector-valued function \n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle  \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\:   \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\:  \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle,\n\\qquad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi), \\quad \\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi],\n$$\n\nrepresents a parametrization of our sphere.\n\n**Watch out!** There are many possible parameterizations of the given sphere. Here, we found one of them." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173182 1064 "Recall that if $(\\rho, \\theta, \\phi)$ are the *spherical* polar coordinates of a point $P$ in the three-dimensional space, then its Cartesian coordinates $(x,y,z)$ can be found using the following formulas:\n\n$$\nx = \\rho\\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad y = \\rho\\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\qquad z = \\rho\\cos\\phi\n$$\n\n![](images/92/925aae5386657b35b3214db2a5348fc88872faec5ef63bc6237deed6e041f3de.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's find a parametrization of the sphere whose cartesian equation is\n\n$$\nx^2+y^2+z^2=1\n$$\n\nThis equation represents a unit sphere (i.e., a sphere of radius $1$) centered at the origin.\n\nGiven a point $P$ on the sphere, the variable $\\rho$ represents the *radial* distance between the origin and $P$. Since every point on the sphere is a distance of $1$ from $O$, we must have $\\rho =1$ at every point on the sphere.\n\nTherefore, for any point $(x,y,z)$ on our surface, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(θ, ϕ) &= ⟨x, y, z⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨ρ\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\sin θ\\sin ϕ, ρ\\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨1 \\cdot \\cos θ\\sin ϕ, 1 \\cdot \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, 1 \\cdot \\cos ϕ⟩ \\\\\n&= ⟨\\cos θ\\sin ϕ, \\sin θ\\sin ϕ, \\cos ϕ⟩\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi)$ and $\\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]$.\nAs a result, the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{f}(\\theta,\\phi) = \\big\\langle \\cos\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\sin\\theta\\sin\\phi, \\: \\cos\\phi \\big\\rangle, \\qquad \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi), \\quad \\phi \\in [-\\pi,\\pi]\n$$\n\nrepresents a parametrization of our sphere.\n\n**Watch out!** There are many possible parameterizations of the given sphere. Here, we found one of them." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173192 1064 "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve\n\n$$\nx=x(y)\\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y$.\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve\n\n$$\nz=z(y)\\geq 0\n$$\n\nin the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173192 1064 "Consider the point $(x(y),y,0)$ that lies on the curve $x=x(y)\\geq 0$ for $y\\in [a,b]$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $y$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/98/981ed341198c54e5dffeae770d13b3d46222ad429d0315ecd057b43995b4c0d6.png)\n\nThe pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $x(y)$ with parameter $y.$\n\nUsing arguments similar to those seen earlier, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[ \n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: x(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]\n\nSimilarly, the rotation of a point $(0,y,z(y))$ on the curve $z=z(y)\\geq 0$ in the $yz$-plane about the $y$-axis can be visualized as follows:\n\n![](images/59/5936d54e55c151ec2d9786a3ce2bd807970a71c10a21974562fd601bf50dfd37.png)\n\nThe corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\cos\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nAs mentioned previously, neither of these parameterizations is unique.\n\n**Note:** Since a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\\[\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle z(y) \\sin\\theta, \\: y, \\: z(y) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad y\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi). \n\\]" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173195 1064 "A connected region $D$ is **simply connected** if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nWhen we have a connected *plane* region (e.g. a region in the $xy$-plane), determining whether it's simply connected is a little simpler: we just need to check whether the region contains holes.\n\nPlane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nSo, if a connected plane region does not contain any holes, then it is simply connected.\n\nMore precisely, a connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D$." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173195 1064 "A connected region $D$ is **simply connected** if every simple closed curve in $D$ can be contracted to a point.\n\n![](images/7d/7d6aea1a62fb290702630ef95ec86f4119e7c8fdd74b8148b62b66e5ac871b58.png)\n\nWhen we have a connected *plane* region (e.g. a region in the $xy$-plane), determining whether it's simply connected is a little simpler: we just need to check whether the region contains holes. \n\nPlane regions containing holes are not simply connected, since some simple closed curves cannot be contracted to a point:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c3e7a60ff0dd917cf6c0b293dae7510e457a0455629add1c54cadac65248b7.png)\n\nSo, if a connected plane region does not contain any holes, then it is simply connected.\n\nMore precisely, a connected *plane* region $D$ is simply connected if every simple closed curve in $D$ encloses only points in $D.$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173207 1064 "Recall that for a path $C$ in the $xy$-plane, the arc length $L$ of $C$ can be computed as the line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C$:\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is defined parametrically as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle, \\quad a\\leq t \\leq b\n$$\n\nthen we can also calculate the arc length of $C$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\mid \\mid \\mathbf r'(t) \\mid \\mid \\,\\textrm d t = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L$, we say that the **arc-length element** $\\textrm ds$ for a parametrically defined curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,\n$$\n\nGeometrically, we can think of $\\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t$. By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C$. We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173207 1064 "Recall that for a path $C$ in the $xy$-plane, the arc length $L$ of $C$ can be computed as the line integral of unity with respect to arc length over $C\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nNow, if $C$ is defined parametrically as \n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle, \\quad a\\leq t \\leq b ,\n$$\n\nthen we can also calculate the arc length of $C$ as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b}||\\mathbf r'(t)||\\,\\textrm d t = \\int_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ we say that the **arc-length element** $\\textrm ds$ for a parametrically defined curve is given by\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d t\\,.\n$$\n\nGeometrically, we can think of $\\textrm ds$ as representing the change in arc length that arises due to a vanishingly small change in $t.$ By summing (integrating) all of these small changes, we arrive at the arc length of $C.$ We'll explore this in more detail at the end of the lesson.\n\nThe idea of an arc length element is useful when we want to think about line integrals in different coordinate systems. It also illustrates a fundamental relationship between the arc length of a path and a line integral of a function along that path." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173208 1064 "Now suppose that a curve $C$ is defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2\n$$\n\nWe've already seen that a polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta$, where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula for the arc length of a polar curve, the arc length of $C$ can be calculated as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as the line integral\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L$, the arc-length element $\\textrm ds$ for a curve can also be defined using polar coordinates as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll build up a geometric understanding of this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's use this result to calculate some line integrals across polar curves." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173208 1064 "Now suppose that a curve $C$ is defined using plane polar coordinates as\n\n$$\nr = r(\\theta), \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nWe've already seen that a polar curve is just a special type of parametric curve in the parameter $\\theta,$ where\n\n$$\nx = r(\\theta)\\cos\\theta, \\qquad y = r(\\theta)\\sin\\theta, \\qquad \\theta_1\\leq\\theta\\leq\\theta_2.\n$$\n\nUsing the usual formula for the arc length of a polar curve, the arc length of $C$ can be calculated as\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{\\theta_1}^{\\theta_2}\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nwhich is the same as the line integral\n\n$$\nL = \\displaystyle\\int\\limits_C \\, \\textrm{d}s.\n$$\n\nBy comparing our two expressions for $L,$ the arc-length element $\\textrm ds$ for a curve can also be defined using polar coordinates as follows:\n\n$$\n\\textrm ds = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm d \\theta\n$$\n\nWe'll build up a geometric understanding of this result at the end of the lesson. But for now, let's use this result to calculate some line integrals across polar curves." 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173209 1064 "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r$.\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t$. This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r$, where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P'$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t)\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y$, respectively, corresponding to the change in $t$.\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r$. Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\left.(Δs)^{2} \\mid \\approx \\mid \\mid Δr \\mid \\right|^{2}; = (Δx)^{2} + (Δy)^{2}; \\approx (\\frac{dx}{dt}Δt)^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt}Δt)^{2}; = (\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2}(Δt)^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}(Δt)^{2}; = [(\\frac{dx}{dt})^{2} + (\\frac{dy}{dt})^{2}](Δt)^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s \\approx \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0$, we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173209 1064 "Let's now build up a geometric understanding of what the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ means. First, we'll consider the case of a parametric curve defined as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\langle x(t),\\, y(t)\\rangle.\n$$\n\nSuppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/2b/2b302806eef01fd6e8160ee6e3183959537c0e9f5c6907238a2eea1d208d3da7.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta t$ in the parameter $t.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/0b/0b1eb1dcc7be637e4e6024b5a8e9e13a4ea5c3e7439be7a2e3083f5ec274ef71.png)\n\nAccording to the diagram we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta x = x(t+\\Delta t) - x(t), \\qquad \\Delta y = y(t+\\Delta t) - y(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\Delta x$ and $\\Delta y$ are the changes in $x$ and $y,$ respectively, corresponding to the change in $t.$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, these quantities can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\Delta x \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t, \\qquad \\Delta y \\approx \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\Delta t.\n$$\n\nThe change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Therefore, by the Pythagorean theorem, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx ||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & ={(Δx)}^{2}+{(Δy)}^{2} \\\\  & \\approx {(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}Δt)}^{2} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}(Δt)^{2} \\\\  & =[{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}](Δt)^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s\n\n\\approx\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\Delta t.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta t \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d x}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2  + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d t}\\right)^2}\\, \\textrm d t\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173210 1064 "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve\n\n$$\nr= r(\\theta)\n$$\n\ndefined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r$.\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta$. This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r$, where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P'$, as shown below.\n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r$, and\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r$.\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'$. The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r$. Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.(Δs)^{2} \\mid \\approx \\mid \\mid Δr \\mid \\right|^{2}; \\approx (Δr)^{2} + (rΔθ)^{2}; = Δr^{2} + r^{2}(Δθ)^{2}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n(Δs)^{2} \\mid \\approx (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}(Δθ)^{2} + r^{2}(Δθ)^{2}; = r^{2}(Δθ)^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}(Δθ)^{2}; = [r^{2} + (\\frac{dr}{dθ})^{2}](Δθ)^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0$, we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s$:\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173210 1064 "Now, let's derive the formula for the arc length element $\\textrm d s$ for a curve defined using plane polar coordinates. We will use a similar geometric approach.\n\nConsider a curve $r= r(\\theta)$ defined using polar coordinates. Suppose that the point $P$ lies on this curve and has position vector $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/7f/7fa77b8d4ad159967f917b438096f472cbd7f541495847e11b919a6843e6259b.png)\n\nNow consider a small change $\\Delta \\theta$ in the parameter $\\theta.$ This change gives a new point $P'$ on the curve with position vector $\\mathbf r + \\Delta\\mathbf r,$ where $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ gives the total change (as a vector) in moving from $P$ to $P',$ as shown below. \n\n![](images/10/105112a2bb993a13282c5f36190ca99a46d80a22a4203f653f7f67060e486d48.png)\n\nFrom here, we see that the vector $\\Delta \\mathbf r$ can be approximated as the vector addition of two components:\n\n- a vector of length $\\Delta r$ acting parallel to $\\mathbf r,$ and\n\n- a vector of length $r\\Delta \\theta$ acting perpendicular to $\\mathbf r.$\n\n![](images/08/080fa1112410328ba369e1c2e5d70fc2e5ddba97606a2fdeaa90d61e3e20d874.png)\n\nNote that $\\Delta r$ is simply the difference of the radial lengths between the points $P$ and $P'.$ The expression $r\\Delta \\theta$ comes from the formula for the arc length of a sector of a circle.\n\nNow, the change in arc length $\\Delta s$ can be approximated as the magnitude of $\\Delta \\mathbf r.$ Since our two components are perpendicular, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx ||Δr|{|}^{2} \\\\  & \\approx (Δr)^{2}+(rΔ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =Δ{r}^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the fact that a difference quotient can be approximated as derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\Delta r = r(\\theta + \\Delta\\theta) - r(\\theta) = \\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into our expression for $(\\Delta s)^2$ above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(Δs)^{2} & \\approx {(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & ={r}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}(Δ\\theta )^{2} \\\\  & =[{r}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}r}{\\text{d}\\theta })}^{2}](Δ\\theta )^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\Delta s = \\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\Delta\\theta.\n$$\n\nIf we imagine the limiting case where $\\Delta \\theta \\to 0,$ we arrive at the arc length element $\\textrm d s\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\textrm d s\n\n=\n\n\\sqrt{r^2 + \\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm d r}{\\textrm d \\theta}\\right)^2}\\textrm d\\theta\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173259 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S =  \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v  \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution.\n\nSuppose that the curve [math]y=f(x)[/math] for [math]x \\in [a,b][/math] is rotated [math]2\\pi[/math] radians about the [math]x[/math]-axis. The corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad (x,\\theta) \\in D, \n\\end{align*}\n\nwhere [math]D= \\big\\{ (x,\\theta) \\: : \\: x \\in[a,b], \\: \\theta \\in [0,2\\pi) \\big\\}.[/math] How can we find the area of this surface?\n\nFirst, we now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial x}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial x} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 1, \\:f'(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[10pt]\n\n\\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \\bigg\\langle \\dfrac{\\partial x}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial y}{\\partial \\theta}, \\: \\dfrac{\\partial z}{\\partial \\theta} \\bigg\\rangle \n= \\big\\langle 0, \\: -f(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nNext, the fundamental vector product is\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \n&= \n\\begin{vmatrix}\n\\mathbf{i} & \\mathbf{j} & \\mathbf{k} \\\\\n1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\\n0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta\n\\end{vmatrix} \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\bigg\\langle \\begin{vmatrix} f'(x) \\cos\\theta & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ -f(x) \\sin\\theta & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: -\\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\sin\\theta \\\\ 0  & f(x) \\cos\\theta \\end{vmatrix}, \\: \\begin{vmatrix} 1 & f'(x) \\cos\\theta \\\\ 0 & -f(x) \\sin\\theta  \\end{vmatrix} \\bigg\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x)\\cos^2\\theta+f(x)f'(x)\\sin^2\\theta, \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\big\\langle f(x)f'(x), \\: -f(x) \\cos\\theta, \\:- f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle\n\\end{align*}\n\nand\n\n\\begin{align*}\n\\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|\n&= \\sqrt{\\big( f(x)f'(x) \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x) \\cos\\theta \\big)^2 + \\big( -f(x)\\sin\\theta \\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 \\big( \\cos^2\\theta + \\sin^2\\theta \\big) } \\\\[3pt]\n&= \\sqrt{\\big(f(x)\\big)^2\\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 + \\big(f(x)\\big)^2 } \\\\[3pt]\n&= f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 }.\n\\end{align*}\n\nTherefore, the surface area is\n\n\\begin{align*}\nA \n&= \\iint\\limits_S \\,  \\text{d}S \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\iint\\limits_D  \\left\\|  \\mathbf{r}'_y \\times \\mathbf{r}'_\\theta \\right\\|  \\text{d}x\\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} \\int_{0}^{2\\pi} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}\\theta \\: \\text{d}x \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\int_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\text{d}\\theta \\\\[5pt]\n&= \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2} \\: \\text{d}x \\cdot \\big[ \\theta \\big]_{0}^{2\\pi}  \\\\[5pt]\n&= 2\\pi \\int_{a}^{b} f(x)\\sqrt{1 + \\big(f'(x)\\big)^2 } \\: \\text{d}x.\n\\end{align*}" 13194139533442 17592186045585 true] [17592186173259 1064 "Recall that if $S$ is a surface parametrized by a differentiable vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(u,v) = \\big\\langle x(u,v), \\: y(u,v), \\: z(u,v) \\big\\rangle\n$$\n\ndefined over a region $D$ in the $uv$-plane, then the surface area of $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_{S} \\: \\textrm{d}S = \\iint\\limits_{D} \\| \\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v \\| \\: \\textrm{d}A\n$$\n\nwhere $\\mathbf{r}'_ u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v$ is the fundamental vector product of the surface.\n\nLet's apply this formula to find the surface area generated by a surface of revolution." 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173260 1064 "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x$.\n\nIn general, given a curve\n\n$$\ny=y(x)\\geq 0\n$$\n\nfor $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obtain different parameterizations of our surface of revolution. For example, the following is also a valid parameterization:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi)\n$$" 13194139533442 17592186045585 false] [17592186173260 1064 "Consider the point $(x,y(x),0)$ that lies on the curve $y=y(x)$ in the $xy$-plane. Rotating this point by $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis generates a circle, as shown below.\n\n![](images/86/86cbc06bd3730430665744216a702ed29cc75acae33a569e00636ee53149065d.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the pair $(\\textrm{circle}_1,\\textrm{circle}_2)$ denotes any parametrization of a circle of radius $y(x)$ with parameter $x.$\n\nIn general, given a curve $y=y(x)\\geq 0$ for $x \\in [a,b]$ that is rotated $2\\pi$ radians about the $x$-axis, the corresponding surface of revolution can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r} = \\big\\langle x, \\: y(x) \\cos\\theta, \\: y(x) \\sin\\theta \\big\\rangle, \\quad x\\in[a,b], \\quad \\theta\\in [0,2\\pi).\n$$\n\nSince a parametrization of a circle is not unique, we can obta